#!/usr/bin/env python3
# Second EN patch: final tightening to land in 4800-5400 and fix ranges.
import json

PATH = 'specs/hist_w56_en.json'
P = {}

P[(0,3)] = "How long should a constitution be? The first answer: one hundred fifty-eight articles in six sections, ready by October and November of nineteen ninety-one. The decisive impulse was the constitutional law 'On the Foundations of State Independence' of August thirty-first, nineteen ninety-one, seventeen articles. In spring nineteen ninety-two came a second version of one hundred forty-nine articles, then a third of one hundred thirty-seven. For the nationwide discussion, a text of one hundred twenty-seven articles was published. The draft was first compiled in Russian, then translated into Uzbek in July and August of nineteen ninety-two. Every version was read and corrected by hand by the chairman; as Academician Orazayev recalled, the President read the draft again and again."
P[(0,4)] = "Let us put the whole journey on one line. June twentieth, nineteen ninety: the Declaration of Independence, and the decision to write a constitution. August thirty-first, nineteen ninety-one: the constitutional law on the foundations of state independence, seventeen articles. October to November nineteen ninety-one: the first draft, one hundred fifty-eight articles. September twenty-sixth, nineteen ninety-two: the draft is published for the nation. November twenty-first, nineteen ninety-two: a second publication, a precedent of two-stage discussion. And finally, December eighth, nineteen ninety-two: the eleventh session of the Supreme Council adopts the Constitution. Each step narrowed the text and widened the circle of people involved."
P[(0,5)] = "Who gets to shape a constitution? In Uzbekistan, everyone. From late September to early December nineteen ninety-two, the draft was debated in newspapers, on radio and television, in collectives and student classrooms. Letters came from scientists and pensioners, workers and dehkans, military and religious figures. More than six thousand proposals arrived; the commission alone received more than a thousand letters. The result: corrections to sixty of the one hundred twenty-seven articles, four articles excluded, several new ones added. On November twenty-first the draft was published a second time, a precedent of two-stage nationwide discussion."
P[(0,6)] = "Adoption day was no formality. On December eighth, nineteen ninety-two, the deputies made about eighty changes and clarifications. The terms of the Constitutional, Supreme and Higher Economic Courts were cut from ten years to five. Article sixty-four, today sixty-five, gained the words that motherhood and childhood are under the protection of the state, proposed by deputy Khalimakhon Usmanova. Article twenty-six fixed the presumption of innocence, article twenty-seven the secrecy of correspondence. The Arbitration Court became the Economic Court, and the parliament was named the Oliy Majlis. The commission, meanwhile, rejected bicameralism and mandatory union membership."
P[(0,7)] = "A typical exam question: prove that the Constitution was a people's law. Step one: on September twenty-sixth, nineteen ninety-two, the draft was published in the press, open to everyone. Step two: the debate ran in newspapers, on radio and television, in collectives and student auditoriums. Step three: more than six thousand proposals arrived, and more than a thousand letters to the commission alone. Step four: the response was real, corrections to sixty of one hundred twenty-seven articles, four articles removed. Step five: on November twenty-first the draft was published a second time, a precedent of two-stage discussion. A law debated letter by letter by the whole nation, that is what people's law means."
P[(1,1)] = "Where does the story of Uzbek courts begin? In a text most people know as scripture. The Avesta, one of the ancient cultural and legal sources of the region, already contained rules concerning judicial cases. Three tasks stand out, and they are on the screen: the court determined the guilt of the perpetrator, the validity of the claim, and the punishment. Guilt, claim, punishment, the same three questions every modern court still asks. So the story of Uzbek justice starts not with statutes, but with the Avesta."
P[(1,3)] = "What changed with Muslim law? Classification, first: crimes were now classified from religious and ideological positions, so the question of what kind of act this was got its answer through faith. And structure, second: a single judge handled all criminal and family-inheritance cases. No division into criminal chambers and family chambers, one figure, one bench, full jurisdiction. Contrast this with the specialized systems of the empire and the Soviets: from one judge for everything, to a whole architecture of courts."
P[(1,5)] = "A classic test question, so answer it like a lawyer. Who could pass a death sentence in the khanates? Step one: recall who ran justice. The emirs and khans stood at the head of judicial power and held the public proceedings. Step two: recall the judges beneath them, the kushbegi, the beks, the qazi-kalon and the qazi-askar. Step three: recall the limit, their powers covered everything except the right of life and death. Step four: draw the conclusion. For the death penalty the permission of the khan, the emir, was required, which means only the khan could pass a death sentence. Say it exactly that way in the exam."
P[(2,1)] = "Follow the empire's steps. Eighteen sixty-five: the Temporary Provision defines the procedure for introducing Russian justice. Eighteen sixty-seven: the Draft Provision on the Organization of the Judicial Branch, in three parts, introduces a two-tier imperial system. June twelfth, eighteen eighty-six: the emperor approves the Provision on the Administration of the Turkestan Region, from now on the main legal document of the region. Eighteen ninety-seven to ninety-nine: the reforms create a judicial chamber, the highest instance, with control functions. And from eighteen ninety-eight to nineteen seventeen, the fourth stage adapts the system to the Judicial Statutes of the empire."
P[(2,4)] = "Now watch Soviet procedure being built, code by code. On August first, nineteen twenty-two, the first systematic Code of Criminal Procedure came into force. The code of nineteen twenty-six established judicial control over detention and the dismissal of cases, in articles two hundred one, two hundred two and two hundred forty-four. The code of nineteen twenty-nine simplified the procedure, but allowed the court itself to draw up the indictment. Finally, on the basis of the Fundamentals of Criminal Justice of nineteen fifty-eight, a new code was approved on May twenty-first, nineteen fifty-nine, and entered practice on January first, nineteen sixty."
P[(2,5)] = "Examiners love this arc, so rehearse it as a chain, from nineteen seventeen to nineteen sixty. December nineteen seventeen: the decree suspends the imperial courts; people's courts and tribunals take over. August first, nineteen twenty-two: the first systematic Code of Criminal Procedure enters force. Nineteen twenty-six: judicial control over detention is fixed in articles two hundred one, two hundred two and two hundred forty-four. Nineteen twenty-nine: the process is simplified, but the court may draw up the indictment itself. May twenty-first, nineteen fifty-nine: a new code is approved, and from January first, nineteen sixty, it governs practice. Decree, code, control, simplification, new code: memorize the chain. Five links, five dates, one story."
P[(3,1)] = "On August sixteenth, nineteen thirty-eight, the second session of the Supreme Soviet of the USSR adopted the law 'On the Judicial System of the USSR, Union and Autonomous Republics': eight chapters, eighty articles, built on the Constitution of nineteen thirty-six. The law split the courts in two. The all-Union courts: the Supreme Court of the USSR, military tribunals, railway and water transport courts. The courts of the Union republics: people's, regional and Supreme Courts. People's judges were elected by district citizens for three years, from the age of twenty-three, while regional and Supreme Courts were elected for five. At the top, the Plenum of the Supreme Court met no less than once every two months."
P[(3,2)] = "Now map that law onto the republic. In the second half of nineteen thirty-nine, the Uzbek SSR had two hundred twenty-six people's courts; only one was closed, for lack of specialists. Two hundred twenty-five people's judges served; just seventeen of them had higher education, and forty-six were women. Above them stood five regional courts, in Tashkent, Samarkand, Fergana, Khorezm and Bukhara, plus the Surkhandarya District Court with sixty-eight judges. Read the table as a diagnosis: the network was wide, but the profession was thin."
P[(3,4)] = "On June twenty-second, nineteen forty-one, the very day of Germany's invasion, two regulations were adopted: 'On the State of War' and 'On Military Tribunals.' Their rules were brutal in their simplicity. The trial had to begin within twenty-four hours after the accused was brought to trial. The death sentence was announced after seventy-two hours. Cassation was prohibited; a verdict could change only in the supervisory procedure. Until nineteen forty-three, tribunals had three permanent members without people's assessors, appointed, not elected. Cases were heard mostly behind closed doors. Yet execution could be postponed until the end of combat operations: the convicted washed away their guilt with blood at the front."
P[(3,6)] = "Let us turn the war years into a cause-and-effect chain. The cause: June twenty-second, nineteen forty-one, Germany invades, and the two war regulations are adopted the same day. The immediate effects: trials within twenty-four hours, death sentences announced after seventy-two, cassation prohibited. The administrative effects: the decree of July fifteenth cuts staff and courts, twenty percent in the countryside, twenty-five percent in cities, twenty-eight courts gone by nineteen forty-two. The response: education, the Law Institute reopens, courses start in Tashkent and Samarkand. The outcome: by the end of nineteen forty-five, only fifty-six lawyers with higher education remain in the people's courts. War made justice faster, harsher, and thinner."
P[(4,1)] = "Here is Azizov's whole classification on one screen. Stage one, nineteen ninety-one to two thousand: the transitional period, forming the foundations of national statehood. Stage two, two thousand one to two thousand nine: democratic renewal. Stage three, twenty ten to twenty sixteen: deepening democratic reforms, about fifty laws. Stage four, from twenty seventeen: the Strategy of Actions. Four stages, four ambitions. Notice the rhythm, roughly a decade each, and each one answering the unfinished business of the one before it. The dates themselves are a favorite test question. Learn them as a rhythm, not as a list."
P[(4,2)] = "Stage one, nineteen ninety-one to two thousand: the transitional period. Its legal pillars: the constitutional law 'On the Foundations of State Independence' of August thirty-first, nineteen ninety-one, seventeen articles, the legal foundation of independence; then the Constitution of nineteen ninety-two; and then entirely new branches of law. Between nineteen ninety-four and two thousand eight, fifteen codes were adopted. Think of the scale: a state that had inherited Soviet law rewrote its own foundations, constitution first, codes after."
P[(4,5)] = "Who writes the laws in stage four? Increasingly, everyone. After the President's address of December twenty-third, twenty seventeen, the 'My Opinion' portal was launched, with four hundred forty collective appeals published. On the Ministry of Justice portal, about eight thousand acts were considered, including five hundred fifty-seven bills and two thousand three hundred eighty draft government resolutions, and thirty-five thousand proposals. Youth gained a voice: a Youth Parliament under the Legislative Chamber, formalized on June twenty-ninth, twenty twenty. And on February twenty-seventh, twenty twenty, a Parliamentary Commission was established to monitor the Sustainable Development Goals up to twenty thirty."
P[(5,3)] = "Two conventions, two different jobs. Convention twenty-nine, from nineteen thirty, defines forced labor and lists its exceptions; it bans forced labor in general. Convention one hundred five, from nineteen fifty-seven, goes further and demands its immediate abolition in five cases. As a means of political coercion or punishment for political opinions. As a method of mobilizing labor for economic development. As a means of labor discipline. As punishment for participation in strikes. And as a means of racial, social, national or religious discrimination. If the exam asks which convention bans forced labor as a tool of discipline, the answer is one hundred five."
P[(5,4)] = "Where does Uzbekistan stand? As of January first, twenty twenty-one, it had ratified seventeen ILO conventions, eight of them fundamental, and one protocol: Convention twenty-nine and Convention one hundred five on forced labor, Convention one eighty-two on the worst forms of child labor, Convention one thirty-eight on minimum age, and the Protocol of twenty fourteen to Convention twenty-nine, ratified on June twenty-fifth, twenty nineteen, in force from September sixteenth, twenty twenty. The UN covenants of nineteen sixty-six were ratified too. But as Narbaeva stressed at the International Labour Conference, ratification is only fifty percent of the solution; the rest is implementation."
P[(5,7)] = "Let us practice the definition. Teachers in a district are ordered to the cotton fields, and those who refuse risk losing pay. Forced labor or not? Run the four elements. Element one: work or service, yes, cotton picking is work. Element two: any person, yes, teachers are persons, and the convention covers everyone. Element three: involuntary nature, yes, this is an order, not a choice. Element four: threat of any penalty, yes, and the penalty need not be criminal, losing pay is enough. All four elements are present, so the conclusion follows: this is forced labor under Convention twenty-nine. Learn the method: check, check, check, check, conclude."
P[(6,1)] = "Who fights forced labor today? Three types of mechanisms work in parallel. First, the collegial bodies: the National Commission on Combating Trafficking in Persons and Forced Labor, the Coordination Council on Child Labor, and the Republican Tripartite Commission on Social and Labor Issues. Second, the labor authorities: state labor inspections, created under the resolution of December thirty-first, twenty eighteen, and authorized to initiate cases and transfer materials to court. Third, civil society: trade unions and non-governmental organizations. Above them all, the Institute of the National Rapporteur reports annually to the President on forced labor."
P[(6,2)] = "Watch the institutions accumulate across three periods. The first, nineteen ninety-two to two thousand seven: Uzbekistan joins the ILO, ratifies conventions twenty-nine and one hundred five, adopts the Labor Code and the Employment Law, creates labor inspections. The second, two thousand eight to twenty sixteen: international criticism produces the anti-trafficking law, resolutions two hundred seven, eighty-two and one hundred thirty-two, a Rehabilitation Center, and the Coordinating Council on Child Labor in twenty thirteen. The third, from twenty seventeen: criminal liability, a Parliamentary Commission from the Senate, and the National Commission with the National Rapporteur under the decree of July thirtieth, twenty nineteen. In twenty nineteen, two hundred fifty-nine cases were identified, fined seven hundred seventy-two million sums; for the twenty twenty campaign, forty-two officials were held accountable."
P[(6,4)] = "Why was there so much cotton, and so much labor to extract? Bhat's article goes back to the empire. By nineteen eleven and twelve, Turkestan already supplied fifty-two percent of the needs of the Russian textile industry; by nineteen fourteen and fifteen, seventy percent. Between eighteen eighty-six and nineteen hundred, plantations grew from forty-one thousand to ninety-two thousand eight hundred eighty-eight dessiatines, and the railway that reached Tashkent in eighteen ninety-eight opened Central Asia to Russian capital. Uzbekistan later became the second largest producer and exporter, over eight hundred thousand tons annually, about sixty percent of Central Asia's cotton. The ecological price: irrigating one million four hundred seventy thousand hectares almost destroyed the Aral Sea, which kept fifteen percent of its volume and lost twenty-four species of endemic fish."
P[(6,7)] = "The last walkthrough, and the deepest question: why did child labor become mass-scale? Step one: the state set cotton quotas, pushing production above what the market would choose. Step two: without market incentives, someone must be compelled to work; coercion becomes a tool of policy. Step three: demechanization, with machine harvesting collapsing from two-thirds to ten percent, multiplied the demand for cheap hands. Step four: the khokimiyats organized the mobilization of children through the schools, quotas passed down to the individual child. Conclusion: systemic child labor was not a survival of tradition; it was produced by a command economy. Without agrarian reform, Bhat concludes, the problem cannot be solved."

d = json.load(open(PATH))
for (ci, bi), txt in P.items():
    d['chapters'][ci]['blocks'][bi]['narration'] = txt
json.dump(d, open(PATH, 'w'), ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)
print('patched', len(P), 'narrations')
